In article <LCL.92May31132112@splinter.coe.northeastern.edu> lcl@meceng.coe.northeastern.edu writes:
>1) In a recent game, I was unable to pick up anything more.  Oh, goody!
>Here comes a Hill Giant!  Killed it, ate it, strength increased, but
>still couldn't pick up the rest of the gold and gems.  Huh?!??

It all depends on how much your strength increased, as well as other factors
such as what your experience level is.  If you're a high enough experience
level, you strength may be able to increase from say 17 to say 18/** 
without affecting how much you can carry.  Consult the WCST Spoilers for
an exact method of computing how much you can carry.

>2) Same game, with a ring of polymorph control on, stepped on a
>polymorph trap.  When asked what I wanted to become, typed "Vampire
>lord", but it wouldn't take it.  Then tried "vampire lord", but it
>still didn't recognize it and changed me to another human.  Pooh.
>What did I do wrong?

When you are asked what you want to polymorph into, always specify the
name of the monster with no caps.  I.E. "vampire lord" is correct.  You're
assuming that because it turned you into a new man/woman, the game didn't
understand what you typed.  This is not the case.  Each time you polymorph,
there is a chance that the control will fail and you will be polymorphed
into a new man/woman.  If you keep specifying a nonexistant monster to
polymorph into, eventually the game will just randomly polymorph you 
(after ~3-4 tries, I think).

>5)Also for 3.1:  There's got to be a way of knowing your prayer
>time-out.  Either it could be listed as a stat in the same way how
>hungry you are is listed, or could be known by applying a rosary or an
>hourglass.  However, I personally would like to see more than merely
>insufficient time or sufficient time; I'd like to have actual numbers
>shown.  Possibly in conjunction with a blessed luckstone?  Better
>still, and my preference, just list it as one more item on the stat
>line.  A prayer time-out could be shown in the same way HP or power
>is:  457(600) if you have yet to wait, 582(600) if you've waited more
>than long enough, 12549(600) if you still have yet to pray!

It's random.  In general, the best way to keep from getting your god
angry is to turn on the timer.  This will put an entry on the status
line that specifies how many moves you've made.  Then just make sure
that at least 300-600 turns have gone by since your last prayer and
you'll generally be all right.  (3000 turns if you're the Hand of
Elbereth - you're expected to be able to make it on your own...)

>6) From previous games:  If instead of giving a priestess 400*level in
>gold, and magnanimously give her everything you've got (say, 56,988 gp
>instead of 4400 gp) she doesn't recognize the hugeness of the gift and
>you go away relatively empty-handed, or so I remember.  Why?  Or did I
>miss something?

If I recall, you do get clairvoyance (automatic mapping) for a number
of turns if you do this (or is that if you give 600*xlvl+1 ?).  In any
case, you don't go away empty handed.  Relatively empty-handed?  It
all depends on how you look at it.

>7) Is there anything that determines the frequency and size of shops
>(and beehives and altars and fountains etc), or is it purely random?
>Somehow it seems to run in streaks...

Shake them dice, baby!  It's totally random, which explains why it
sometimes appears to run in streaks.  Random means random, not 
necessarily evenly distributed.

